   375	
   376	**Theorem 9.1.** Fix a finite `N≥0`, constants `c1,c3` with `c3≠0`, and parameters over
   377	the real or complex numbers with
   378	`k_i+k_j≠0`, `l_i+l_j≠0` for every distinct pair `i,j`. Let `a_i,b_i` be arbitrary
   379	independent constants (zero is allowed), and put
   380	
   381	    eta_i = k_i x + l_i y − (c1 k_i + c3 k_i^3)t,
   382	    R_S(k) = product_{i<j in S} (k_i−k_j)/(k_i+k_j).
   383	
   384	Constant phase shifts can be absorbed into both `a_i,b_i`. Define two antisymmetric
   385	matrices on the physical indices by their upper entries
   386	
   387	    B0_ij = (l_i a_i b_j − l_j a_j b_i)/(l_i+l_j),
   388	    C0_ij = (l_i b_i a_j − l_j b_j a_i)/(l_i+l_j).
   389	
   390	For an odd subset `S`, append a dummy index `*` **last**, with
   391	`B0_i*=a_i`, `C0_i*=b_i`. For an even subset use no dummy index. Write the resulting
   392	ordered set as `S*`, and set `Pf(empty)=1`. Then
   393	
   394	    F = sum_{S subset [N]} R_S(k) Pf(B0_{S*}) exp(sum_{i in S} eta_i),
   395	    G = sum_{S subset [N]} R_S(k) Pf(C0_{S*}) exp(sum_{i in S} eta_i)
   396	
   397	satisfy
   398	
   399	    P(D_x,D_t)F·G = 0,       D_y P(D_x,D_t)F·G = 0,
   400	    P = D_t + c1 D_x + c3 D_x^3.
   401	
   402	This is exactly C6's formula when all `b_i≠0`: multiplying row and column i by `b_i`
   403	in the Pfaffian shows `Pf(B0_{S*})=(product b_i)Pf(M_{S*})` and likewise for C0/N.
   404	Unlike the ratio notation `x_i=a_i/b_i`, this expression has **no amplitude
   405	denominator** and is defined without taking limits when any `b_i=0`.
   406	
   407	The only parameter denominators in the displayed solution are the pair sums
   408	`k_i+k_j` and `l_i+l_j`. The proof does not divide by F, G, a determinant, a seed
   409	amplitude, an individual `k_i` or `l_i`, or a multi-index dispersion value.
   410	It therefore remains valid when the recursive ansatz's determinant happens to
   411	vanish, provided the displayed pair sums do not vanish. Distinctness and nonzero
   412	individual wave numbers are needed only to describe generic nondegenerate
   413	solitons, not for the algebraic identity. Tau-function zeros are allowed; no
   414	claim about globally regular logarithmic physical fields is included.
