   416	#### 9.1 Pfaffian conventions and elementary identities
   417	
   418	For an even-dimensional antisymmetric matrix H and ordered column vectors
   419	`v1,...,v(2m)`, define
   420	
   421	    [v1,...,v(2m)]_H =
   422	        Pf( [ H       V ]
   423	            [ −V^T    0 ] ),           V=(v1 ... v(2m)),
   424	    []_H = Pf(H).
   425	
   426	The bottom-right block here is zero. Also write `u∧v = uv^T−vu^T`.
   427	The sign convention is important: in dimension two
   428	`[u,v]_H = −u1 v2 + u2 v1`, independent of H.
   429	
   430	The identities used below are:
   431	
   432	    Pf(H+u∧v) = Pf(H) − [u,v]_H,                         (9.1)
   433	    [V]_{H+u∧v} = [V]_H − [u,v,V]_H,                   (9.2)
   434	    Pf(H)[u,v,w,z]_H =
   435	        [u,v]_H[w,z]_H − [u,w]_H[v,z]_H
   436	        + [u,z]_H[v,w]_H.                              (9.3)
   437	
   438	Here (9.2) includes the empty V and any even number of border vectors.
   439	For (9.1)–(9.2), expand the Pfaffian in its entries: terms with two copies of
   440	the decomposable two-form `u∧v` vanish by alternation, leaving exactly its
   441	linear contribution with the indicated minus sign. Alternatively expand the
   442	bordered matrix along its final two rows.
   443	For (9.3), on the dense set of invertible H the block Pfaffian identity gives
   444	
   445	    [V]_H = Pf(H) Pf(V^T H^(-1) V).
   446	
   447	The Pfaffian of the resulting 4-by-4 antisymmetric matrix is its three-term
   448	pairing expansion, proving (9.3) there. Both sides of (9.3) are polynomials
   449	in the entries of H and the vectors, so the identity also holds at singular H.
   450	This supplies a dimension-independent proof, not an invertibility assumption.
   451	
   452	Differentiating (9.1) gives the rule
   453	
   454	    if H_z = u∧v, then
   455	    d_z [V]_H = −[u,v,V]_H
   456	                + sum of [V]_H with one vector differentiated.      (9.4)
   457	
   458	Linearity handles sums of rank-two derivatives.
   459	
   460	#### 9.2 Universal cubic bilinear lemma
   461	
   462	Let `H=H(X,s)` be any even antisymmetric matrix, and let vectors `r_j`, `j≥0`,
   463	satisfy
   464	
   465	    (r_j)_X = r_(j+1),       (r_j)_s = r_(j+3),
   466	    H_X = r1∧r0,
   467	    H_s = r3∧r0 − 2 r2∧r1.                             (9.5)
   468	
   469	Let v be independent of X and s; it may depend on y. Put
   470	`tau=Pf(H)` and `psi=[r0,v]_H`. Then
   471	
   472	    (D_X^3 − D_s) tau·psi = 0.                         (9.6)
